# git-secrets allowlist.
#
# Deliberately NARROW. Each entry below permits one specific, self-evidently
# fake literal that exists so a test can prove a real credential would never
# be persisted or logged. Any OTHER "ya29." string still trips the scanner,
# which is the point: these tests are about credential handling, so they must
# contain credential-shaped strings, and that must not blind the scanner to a
# real one.
#
# A genuine Google OAuth access token is a couple of hundred characters of
# base64. Every literal below is a short descriptive phrase. If you add a
# fixture, give it an obviously-fake descriptive name and add it here rather
# than loosening the pattern or committing with --no-verify.

# Detection logic, not a secret: the prefixes the credential guard looks for.
value\.startswith\(\("ya29\.", "Bearer ", "eyJ"\)\)

# Test fixtures proving a token is never written to durable session state.
ya29\.persisted-and-therefore-forbidden
ya29\.super-secret
ya29\.THIS-TOKEN-MUST-NEVER-BE-PERSISTED

# Test fixtures for the wiring and session-manager suites.
ya29\.a0-fake-user-access-token
ya29\.fake-workforce-principal-access-token
